Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Three gaps that would have cost the LinkedIn push.

1. Every page shared one social card. A shared calculator previewed as
   'The Forge Line', wasting the click on the platform that renders the
   image before the title. /api/og now draws the page's own title at
   1200x630 in the site palette, with length-adaptive type and truncation
   rather than overflow; query text is drawn as text, never markup. Wired
   into all tools, products, solutions, disciplines, pricing, about and
   home, with matching twitter:image.

2. No /about page. LinkedIn traffic arrives asking whether the person is
   real; the site answered with schema no human reads. The page now says
   what the site is, what is deliberately NOT claimed (no customers, no
   case studies, three of four products repo-only, build logs pending),
   the boundaries held on purpose, and five links that let a reader verify
   any of it. Nothing unverifiable was invented; operator-only detail is
   left as a marked TODO.

3. The homepage still said 'three doors' and never mentioned Solutions or
   Pricing — it predated the new architecture. It is now a directory of
   the six sections in the order a visitor needs them, deriving from the
   same registries as the navigation so it cannot drift again.

Verified: OG endpoint returns a valid 1200x630 PNG, five sampled pages
carry five distinct card URLs, /about resolves in both languages, the
homepage links all six sections, 77/77 pages build.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
